---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Favour Diokpo"
  description="How Favour finds the real problem, defines product behaviour, and designs AI systems people can trust."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-6 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        I turn messy product problems into systems people can actually trust.
      </h1>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          I am a Senior UX Specialist at Ubisoft, and I am building HERA OS.
          The work I enjoy most usually starts before the screen: figuring out
          what is actually broken, what the system should own, what the AI should
          decide, and where a human still needs to stay in control.
        </p>
        <p>
          I do not think good AI product design is about adding a smarter model
          to an old workflow. I think it is about deciding what has to be true
          around that model so a person can trust the work without carrying the
          whole system in their head.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">01</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you find the real problem?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I usually start by asking one thing:
          <strong>what is the user carrying that the product should be carrying instead?</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 card p-6 sm:p-8">
        <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.9fr_1.4fr]">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">The HERA story</p>
            <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
              I thought the problem was managing agents.
              It turned out the problem was everything I had to remember for them.
            </h3>

            <div class="prose-hera mt-5">
              <p>
                AI agents could already ask questions, use tools, and work for long periods.
                But I was still remembering which file was current, what I had already decided,
                what another agent had learned, which version had been reviewed, and whether
                I had answered the same question before.
              </p>
              <p>
                HERA started because I kept feeling that exact problem while working with AI.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-8">
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">1 — What I questioned</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                Why should an intelligent product keep asking me to act as its memory?
                If I already told a friend I went to Ibiza, I expect the next useful question
                to be “who did you go with?”, not “where did you travel?”
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">2 — What I realized</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                The bottleneck was not simply model intelligence. It was trust.
                I needed to know that when the AI interrupted me, it had already checked
                the information I had given it and genuinely needed something only I could decide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">3 — The product decision</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                Make context part of the product, not something hidden inside a prompt.
                Decisions, files, previous work, accepted results and ongoing tasks should have
                stable places the system can inspect and the human can audit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">4 — What that changed</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                HERA became less about “a better chat” and more about an operating environment
                for work: the person can arrive with rough intent, the system turns it into
                structured work, and interruptions only happen when the human genuinely matters.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-8 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          That is usually how I work: I follow the friction until I can describe the burden
          the user should never have been carrying in the first place.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">02</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you decide what the AI should do — and what the product should enforce?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I separate three things that AI products often blur together:
          <strong>software rules, AI judgment, and human judgment.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          If a rule can be guaranteed cheaply in code, I do not want to spend model intelligence
          hoping the AI remembers it. If the answer requires interpretation, research, or synthesis,
          that is where the model is useful. If two valid answers depend on what the human actually
          wants, the product should come back to the human.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow">The product guarantees</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Things that should not depend on the AI “remembering”
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>Which piece of work is active.</li>
            <li>What the AI is allowed to access.</li>
            <li>Whether an answer has already been submitted.</li>
            <li>Whether the current result is still waiting for review.</li>
            <li>How a known capability is found and loaded.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow">The AI decides</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Things that genuinely benefit from intelligence
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>Which context is relevant to inspect.</li>
            <li>What needs research before work can continue.</li>
            <li>How to break a large outcome into bounded work.</li>
            <li>Which specialist or tool is appropriate for the job.</li>
            <li>Whether a low-risk assumption is safe enough to make.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow">The human decides</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Things the system should never quietly invent
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>What outcome actually matters.</li>
            <li>Preferences that materially change the result.</li>
            <li>Whether a risky action is allowed.</li>
            <li>Whether a result is acceptable.</li>
            <li>When the direction itself needs to change.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 card p-6 sm:p-8">
        <p class="eyebrow">A very unglamorous example</p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          More AI was not the fix.
        </h3>
        <div class="prose-hera mt-5 max-w-[52rem]">
          <p>
            During HERA development, two parallel builders exposed a naming mismatch:
            one part of the system stored a Skill name with spaces while another looked
            for the same name with underscores. Digging further showed that the problem
            was not one broken Skill — the entire capability family could silently exist
            without ever being found.
          </p>
          <p>
            I did not solve that by telling the model to “try harder.” I moved the guarantee
            into deterministic lookup so the product checks safe aliases automatically.
            The AI should spend intelligence on the work, not on guessing how a filename was formatted.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">03</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you know an AI system actually works?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I learned the annoying way that <strong>“it exists”</strong> is not the same as <strong>“a user can reach it.”</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6 sm:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">Failure 01</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Green tests. Zero real permission requests.
          </h3>
          <div class="prose-hera mt-5">
            <p>
              HERA's permission work looked healthy in automated testing. Then I forced the team
              to test it through the real product using an actual task a person might want done.
            </p>
            <p>
              The permission engine was never reached. The “real” test had only described the
              behaviour instead of actually exercising it.
            </p>
            <p>
              That changed my definition of proof: real task, real model, real runtime,
              real frontend, and a result a human can actually inspect.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-6 sm:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">Failure 02</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
            More than twenty Skills existed. None had ever run.
          </h3>
          <div class="prose-hera mt-5">
            <p>
              The files were there. The instructions were there. The system still could not find them
              because of a tiny naming mismatch.
            </p>
            <p>
              That failure reinforced a rule I now use constantly:
              <strong>reachability is part of the product.</strong>
              Context, tools, permissions, Skills and previous work do not matter merely because they exist.
              They matter when the right thing can be found at the moment the work needs it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">04</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        How do you actually use AI in your own work?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I do not use AI to outsource product judgment. I use it to compress the work around that judgment.
        </p>
        <p>
          My goal is to make the instruction smaller because the system around the instruction is better.
          In the extreme, I want a user to be able to say something as simple as “build the About page”
          and have the surrounding product already know the relevant decisions, context, permissions,
          workflow, acceptance criteria and review rules. "I know, crazy!"
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6 sm:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">What stays with me</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Product judgment
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>Finding the real problem.</li>
            <li>Deciding the outcome worth pursuing.</li>
            <li>Defining how the system should behave.</li>
            <li>Choosing what must remain human.</li>
            <li>Making the important trade-offs.</li>
            <li>Accepting or rejecting the final result.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-6 sm:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">What I let AI accelerate</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
            The work around the judgment
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted space-y-3">
            <li>Repository and evidence research.</li>
            <li>Exploring multiple approaches quickly.</li>
            <li>Implementation from a bounded specification.</li>
            <li>Adversarial critique of a proposed solution.</li>
            <li>Independent verification.</li>
            <li>Repetitive execution and documentation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-8 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          The surprising thing I learned is that getting an agent to build is often the easy part.
          The difficult part is everything that has to be true <em>before</em> you press Send:
          the context, the rules, the handoffs, the permissions, the evidence, and the definition of done.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">05</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Is this way of thinking specific to AI?
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          No. HERA made the pattern easier to name, but I had been doing versions of the same work
          inside enterprise products for years.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 card p-6 sm:p-8">
        <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr]">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Ubisoft</p>
            <h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
              The systems changed. The questions did not.
            </h3>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-7">
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Resource planning</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                I led research across 1,000+ users, then audited 40+ modal patterns before proposing
                changes. The job was not “make the interface cleaner.” It was understanding why people
                did not trust a system they had to use every day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Hybrid workplace approvals</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                I translated an ambiguous multi-role process into five explicit approval flows,
                with clearer accountability and decision messaging under HR and legal constraints.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Capacity governance</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
                I chose warning-based decision support over hard blocking so the product could surface
                risk without pretending policy should replace human judgment.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-8 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          That is the through-line in my work: find the burden hiding underneath the interface,
          make the rules understandable, and put the right responsibility back into the system.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">What I want to work on next</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Products where behaviour is the hard part.
      </h2>

      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[48rem]">
        <p>
          I am most interested in 0→1 AI products, agentic systems, human–AI workflows,
          and complex software where trust, context, autonomy and control matter more
          than adding another screen.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-t border-line pb-20">
    <div class="container-page reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Explore the work</h2>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
        <a href={withBase('work/hera/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">HERA OS</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">See the product &rarr;</span>
        </a>

        <a href={withBase('work/enterprise-tools/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">Ubisoft Work</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">See the systems &rarr;</span>
        </a>

        <a href={withBase('work/lineage/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">Lineage</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">See the progression &rarr;</span>
        </a>

        <a href={withBase('contact/')} class="card card-hover flex flex-col justify-between p-6">
          <span class="text-base font-medium tracking-tight text-ink">Get in touch</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-muted mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1">Contact &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
